# Line endings. Store everything as LF, and check it out as LF on every platform.
#
# This is not a style preference. The build shell is MSYS2 bash, and a .sh file checked out
# with CRLF fails at its first line with `$'\r': command not found` -- which reads like a
# syntax error in the script rather than what it is. Git's default on Windows
# (core.autocrlf=true) produces exactly that on a fresh clone.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The exceptions: Windows tools that genuinely want CRLF.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.inf text eol=crlf

# Patches must reach `git apply` byte for byte. No conversion, in either direction.
*.patch -text
